<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abarkuh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abarquh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abarkuh is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a> Province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on old routes between Yazd, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. In the 11th century it was granted with Yazd to the Kakuyid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faramurz">Faramurz</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, and it later belonged to the atabegs of Yazd. It is known for the Gonbad-e Ali tomb tower of 1056 and an ancient cypress tree said to be several thousand years old.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abarkuh.jpg" alt="Abarkuh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abarkuh_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
